1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:01,689
Look at this report file.

2
00:00:01,689 --> 00:00:03,417
The first line, I ran by hand.

3
00:00:03,417 --> 00:00:06,123
Every line after that, I didn't touch anything.

4
00:00:06,123 --> 00:00:09,981
My computer ran the script on its own, every minute, on a schedule.

5
00:00:09,981 --> 00:00:13,206
In this video, you'll set that up for any Python script.

6
00:00:13,206 --> 00:00:15,222
On a Mac, on Linux, and on Windows.

7
00:00:15,222 --> 00:00:16,834
It takes about five minutes.

8
00:00:17,534 --> 00:00:21,644
A script that runs itself is the moment automation really clicks.

9
00:00:21,644 --> 00:00:25,437
Backups, reports, reminders, cleaning your downloads folder.

10
00:00:25,437 --> 00:00:28,472
Write it once, schedule it, and forget about it.

11
00:00:28,472 --> 00:00:30,748
Let's do it step by step, from zero.

12
00:00:31,448 --> 00:00:32,680
Here's the difference.

13
00:00:32,680 --> 00:00:35,592
When you run a script by hand, you have to remember.

14
00:00:35,592 --> 00:00:38,952
You open a terminal, you type the command, every single day.

15
00:00:38,952 --> 00:00:40,577
A scheduled script just runs.

16
00:00:40,577 --> 00:00:43,377
At the same time, every day, even when you forget.

17
00:00:43,377 --> 00:00:46,625
And every run leaves a record, so you can check it worked.

18
00:00:47,325 --> 00:00:48,248
Here's the plan.

19
00:00:48,248 --> 00:00:49,805
A small script to schedule.

20
00:00:49,805 --> 00:00:52,688
Then cron, the scheduler built into Mac and Linux.

21
00:00:52,688 --> 00:00:57,879
I'll show you the mistake that breaks almost everyone's first schedule, and how to fix it.

22
00:00:57,879 --> 00:00:59,494
Then Windows Task Scheduler.

23
00:00:59,494 --> 00:01:02,608
And finally, how to make sure it never silently fails.

24
00:01:03,308 --> 00:01:04,337
First, the script.

25
00:01:04,337 --> 00:01:07,594
I have a folder called scripts, with a to do list inside.

26
00:01:07,594 --> 00:01:08,736
Each line is a task.

27
00:01:08,736 --> 00:01:10,565
An x in the brackets means done.

28
00:01:10,565 --> 00:01:12,336
Empty brackets mean still open.

29
00:01:12,336 --> 00:01:18,336
Our script will count the open tasks, and write one line with the date and the count, into a report file.

30
00:01:19,036 --> 00:01:20,343
Here's report dot py.

31
00:01:20,343 --> 00:01:25,388
It opens todo dot t x t, and keeps only the lines that start with empty brackets.

32
00:01:25,388 --> 00:01:28,689
Then it gets the current date and time with datetime.

33
00:01:28,689 --> 00:01:32,115
It builds one line, and appends it to report dot t x t.

34
00:01:32,115 --> 00:01:37,284
The letter a means append, so each run adds a new line instead of erasing the file.

35
00:01:37,984 --> 00:01:39,903
Always run it by hand first.

36
00:01:39,903 --> 00:01:43,056
Inside the folder, python three report dot py.

37
00:01:43,056 --> 00:01:45,866
It prints today's line, three open tasks.

38
00:01:45,866 --> 00:01:48,539
And report dot t x t now has that line.

39
00:01:48,539 --> 00:01:49,704
The script works.

40
00:01:49,704 --> 00:01:51,966
Now let's make it run on its own.

41
00:01:52,666 --> 00:01:55,500
On Mac and Linux, the scheduler is called cron.

42
00:01:55,500 --> 00:01:59,480
It's already installed, and it's always running in the background.

43
00:01:59,480 --> 00:02:02,314
Each user has a list of jobs, called a crontab.

44
00:02:02,314 --> 00:02:04,243
Type crontab dash l, l for list.

45
00:02:04,243 --> 00:02:05,449
No crontab for alex.

46
00:02:05,449 --> 00:02:06,716
So the list is empty.

47
00:02:06,716 --> 00:02:08,163
Let's add our first job.

48
00:02:08,863 --> 00:02:10,204
A cron job is one line.

49
00:02:10,204 --> 00:02:13,119
It starts with five time fields, then the command.

50
00:02:13,119 --> 00:02:13,527
Minute.

51
00:02:13,527 --> 00:02:13,819
Hour.

52
00:02:13,819 --> 00:02:14,810
Day of the month.

53
00:02:14,810 --> 00:02:15,160
Month.

54
00:02:15,160 --> 00:02:16,326
And day of the week.

55
00:02:16,326 --> 00:02:17,434
A star means every.

56
00:02:17,434 --> 00:02:21,049
So five stars means every minute, of every hour, of every day.

57
00:02:21,049 --> 00:02:22,215
Perfect for testing.

58
00:02:22,215 --> 00:02:24,547
Watch how many runs that is in one week.

59
00:02:24,547 --> 00:02:25,597
Over ten thousand.

60
00:02:26,297 --> 00:02:27,665
Now, one important rule.

61
00:02:27,665 --> 00:02:31,655
Cron doesn't run from your folder, and it doesn't know your shortcuts.

62
00:02:31,655 --> 00:02:33,080
So we give it full paths.

63
00:02:33,080 --> 00:02:36,442
Type which python three, to see exactly where Python lives.

64
00:02:36,442 --> 00:02:39,862
Mine is slash user slash local slash bin slash python three.

65
00:02:39,862 --> 00:02:41,287
Yours might be different.

66
00:02:41,287 --> 00:02:42,712
Copy whatever yours says.

67
00:02:42,712 --> 00:02:45,676
And p w d gives the full path of the scripts folder.

68
00:02:46,376 --> 00:02:48,263
Type crontab dash e, e for edit.

69
00:02:48,263 --> 00:02:50,563
It opens your crontab in a text editor.

70
00:02:50,563 --> 00:02:54,042
If it asks you to pick an editor, choose nano, the easiest.

71
00:02:54,042 --> 00:02:58,523
On a Mac, type EDITOR equals nano, then crontab dash e, to get the same one.

72
00:02:58,523 --> 00:03:04,538
On a new line, type the five stars, a space, the full path to Python, and the full path to the script.

73
00:03:04,538 --> 00:03:07,545
At the end, two greater than signs, and a log file.

74
00:03:07,545 --> 00:03:10,965
That saves everything the script prints, including errors.

75
00:03:10,965 --> 00:03:13,560
Two greater than one sends errors there too.

76
00:03:13,560 --> 00:03:14,444
Save and close.

77
00:03:14,444 --> 00:03:17,334
In nano, that's control O, enter, then control X.

78
00:03:18,033 --> 00:03:19,699
Run crontab dash l again.

79
00:03:19,699 --> 00:03:20,765
There's our job.

80
00:03:20,765 --> 00:03:24,762
Cron checks this list every minute, so we only have to wait.

81
00:03:25,462 --> 00:03:27,434
A minute later, let's check the log.

82
00:03:27,434 --> 00:03:28,201
And, an error.

83
00:03:28,201 --> 00:03:29,899
File not found, todo dot t x t.

84
00:03:29,899 --> 00:03:31,433
But the file is right there.

85
00:03:31,433 --> 00:03:32,693
It worked a minute ago.

86
00:03:32,693 --> 00:03:33,460
What happened?

87
00:03:33,460 --> 00:03:35,158
This is the number one mistake.

88
00:03:35,158 --> 00:03:38,883
When cron runs your script, it doesn't start in your scripts folder.

89
00:03:38,883 --> 00:03:40,527
It starts in your home folder.

90
00:03:40,527 --> 00:03:43,156
So open todo dot t x t looks in the wrong place.

91
00:03:43,856 --> 00:03:45,095
The fix is two lines.

92
00:03:45,095 --> 00:03:46,570
Import Path from pathlib.

93
00:03:46,570 --> 00:03:51,821
Then folder equals Path, of underscore underscore file underscore underscore, dot parent.

94
00:03:51,821 --> 00:03:56,836
That's the folder where the script itself lives, no matter where it was started from.

95
00:03:56,836 --> 00:04:01,792
Now we open folder slash todo dot t x t, and write to folder slash report dot t x t.

96
00:04:01,792 --> 00:04:04,388
Use this trick in every script you schedule.

97
00:04:05,087 --> 00:04:07,475
Save it, and wait a couple of minutes.

98
00:04:07,475 --> 00:04:09,736
Now the report file grows by itself.

99
00:04:09,736 --> 00:04:14,951
One line from when I ran it by hand, and then one new line every minute, from cron.

100
00:04:14,951 --> 00:04:16,019
No typing at all.

101
00:04:16,719 --> 00:04:18,663
Every minute was just for testing.

102
00:04:18,663 --> 00:04:22,893
To run it once a day, at nine in the morning, change the first two fields.

103
00:04:22,893 --> 00:04:24,036
Zero for the minute.

104
00:04:24,036 --> 00:04:25,065
Nine for the hour.

105
00:04:25,065 --> 00:04:27,924
Then three stars, any day, any month, any weekday.

106
00:04:27,924 --> 00:04:29,238
Watch the week fill in.

107
00:04:29,238 --> 00:04:31,011
One run a day, at nine o'clock.

108
00:04:31,011 --> 00:04:31,639
Seven runs.

109
00:04:32,340 --> 00:04:39,037
Back in crontab dash e, I replaced the five stars with zero, nine, star, star, star, and saved.

110
00:04:39,037 --> 00:04:41,927
Crontab dash l confirms the new schedule.

111
00:04:42,627 --> 00:04:43,681
Only on work days?

112
00:04:43,681 --> 00:04:45,906
The last field is the day of the week.

113
00:04:45,906 --> 00:04:48,306
Zero is Sunday, one is Monday, and so on.

114
00:04:48,306 --> 00:04:50,648
So one dash five means Monday to Friday.

115
00:04:50,648 --> 00:04:53,048
Thirty, eight, star, star, one dash five.

116
00:04:53,048 --> 00:04:54,512
Eight thirty on weekdays.

117
00:04:54,512 --> 00:04:56,737
Five runs, and the weekend stays free.

118
00:04:57,437 --> 00:04:59,790
Here are the schedules you'll use most.

119
00:04:59,790 --> 00:05:03,831
Star slash fifteen in the minute field means every fifteen minutes.

120
00:05:03,831 --> 00:05:07,210
Zero, star, star, star, star is every hour, on the hour.

121
00:05:07,210 --> 00:05:09,804
Zero nine star star one is Mondays at nine.

122
00:05:09,804 --> 00:05:13,664
Zero zero one star star is midnight on the first of every month.

123
00:05:13,664 --> 00:05:18,310
And you can list hours with commas, like nine and seventeen, for twice a day.

124
00:05:19,010 --> 00:05:19,693
Mistake two.

125
00:05:19,693 --> 00:05:21,400
Swapping the first two fields.

126
00:05:21,400 --> 00:05:24,076
People write nine, zero, thinking nine o'clock.

127
00:05:24,076 --> 00:05:26,410
But the first field is always the minute.

128
00:05:26,410 --> 00:05:29,085
So nine, zero means nine minutes past midnight.

129
00:05:29,085 --> 00:05:30,964
Your report runs while you sleep.

130
00:05:30,964 --> 00:05:31,476
Remember.

131
00:05:31,476 --> 00:05:32,842
Minute first, then hour.

132
00:05:33,542 --> 00:05:34,290
Now Windows.

133
00:05:34,290 --> 00:05:37,907
Cron isn't there, but Windows has Task Scheduler built in.

134
00:05:37,907 --> 00:05:39,840
First, find Python's full path.

135
00:05:39,840 --> 00:05:42,459
Open Command Prompt and type where python.

136
00:05:42,459 --> 00:05:46,138
Copy the path it shows, the one ending in python dot e x e.

137
00:05:46,138 --> 00:05:50,191
Then open the Start menu, search for Task Scheduler, and open it.

138
00:05:50,891 --> 00:05:52,977
Click Create Basic Task, on the right.

139
00:05:52,977 --> 00:05:54,843
Give it a name, like Daily report.

140
00:05:54,843 --> 00:05:55,447
Click next.

141
00:05:55,447 --> 00:05:59,179
For the trigger, choose Daily, and set the start time, say nine A M.

142
00:05:59,179 --> 00:06:00,771
Next, choose Start a program.

143
00:06:00,771 --> 00:06:01,649
Now three boxes.

144
00:06:01,649 --> 00:06:04,888
Program or script, paste the full path to python dot e x e.

145
00:06:04,888 --> 00:06:06,754
Add arguments, type report dot py.

146
00:06:06,754 --> 00:06:09,882
And Start in, paste the full path of your scripts folder.

147
00:06:09,882 --> 00:06:11,145
Click next, and finish.

148
00:06:11,845 --> 00:06:15,333
That Start in box is the Windows version of our cron mistake.

149
00:06:15,333 --> 00:06:17,620
It sets the folder the script starts in.

150
00:06:17,620 --> 00:06:22,937
Leave it empty, and Windows starts somewhere else, and you get the same file not found error.

151
00:06:22,937 --> 00:06:26,254
With the Path trick in our script, you're safe either way.

152
00:06:26,954 --> 00:06:32,526
To test it, find your task in the Task Scheduler Library, right click it, and choose Run.

153
00:06:32,526 --> 00:06:35,030
Then look at the Last Run Result column.

154
00:06:35,030 --> 00:06:39,225
The operation completed successfully, zero x zero, means it worked.

155
00:06:39,225 --> 00:06:42,920
Any other code means the script failed, so check the paths.

156
00:06:43,620 --> 00:06:44,455
Mistake three.

157
00:06:44,455 --> 00:06:47,438
A scheduler can only run while the computer is on.

158
00:06:47,438 --> 00:06:51,078
If your laptop is asleep at nine, cron simply skips that run.

159
00:06:51,078 --> 00:06:59,252
On Windows, open the task's properties, go to the Settings tab, and tick, run task as soon as possible after a scheduled start is missed.

160
00:06:59,252 --> 00:07:02,295
Or pick a time when your computer is usually awake.

161
00:07:02,995 --> 00:07:03,656
Pro tip one.

162
00:07:03,656 --> 00:07:04,759
Always keep the log.

163
00:07:04,759 --> 00:07:09,775
Without it, when a scheduled script fails, it fails silently, and you won't know for weeks.

164
00:07:09,775 --> 00:07:14,129
With two greater than signs and a log file, one look tells you what went wrong.

165
00:07:14,129 --> 00:07:14,791
Pro tip two.

166
00:07:14,791 --> 00:07:18,814
To change or stop a job, use crontab dash e, and edit or delete the line.

167
00:07:18,814 --> 00:07:20,358
Careful with crontab dash r.

168
00:07:20,358 --> 00:07:23,334
It deletes your whole crontab, with no question asked.

169
00:07:24,034 --> 00:07:31,048
And on a Mac, if your script reads files in Desktop, Documents or Downloads, macOS privacy settings may block cron.

170
00:07:31,048 --> 00:07:39,403
The simple fix is to keep your scheduled scripts and their files in their own folder, like scripts in your home folder, just like we did.

171
00:07:40,103 --> 00:07:40,813
Quick recap.

172
00:07:40,813 --> 00:07:42,529
Run the script by hand first.

173
00:07:42,529 --> 00:07:47,025
Use Path of underscore underscore file, dot parent, for every file it opens.

174
00:07:47,025 --> 00:07:51,404
On Mac and Linux, crontab dash e, five time fields, full paths, and a log.

175
00:07:51,404 --> 00:07:52,824
Minute first, then hour.

176
00:07:52,824 --> 00:07:58,800
On Windows, Create Basic Task, the path to python, your script as the argument, and fill in Start in.

177
00:07:59,500 --> 00:08:03,466
Next on Shortcut Lab, the V S Code shortcuts that save hours.

178
00:08:03,466 --> 00:08:08,862
Edit ten lines at once, jump anywhere in a second, and stop reaching for the mouse.

179
00:08:08,862 --> 00:08:10,943
Subscribe, so you don't miss it.
